Let's dive into our next BTLO challenge, shall we? This one will be the investigation called "The Report," and it is intended to mimic a newly established SOC environment.

Question 1) Name the supply chain attack related to Java logging library in the end of 2021 (Format: AttackNickname)
So we start with a Report.zip archive, with password "BTLO". It opens up a PDF report for us, where we can then see on page 15:
"Log4j is a popular Java logging library underlying many third-party applications that was hit with a remote code execution vulnerability in December 2021."

Question 2) Mention the MITRE Technique ID which effected more than 50% of the customers (Format: TXXXX)
Here we go, page 73 shows us that the top answer, with 53.4% affected, is T1059: Command & Scripting Interpreter.

Question 3) Submit the names of 2 vulnerabilities belonging to Exchange Servers (Format: VulnNickname, VulnNickname)
Page 17 gives us: "ProxyLogon and ProxyShell targeted Microsoft Exchange servers and affected a massive number of systems [...]"

Question 4) Submit the CVE of the zero day vulnerability of a driver which led to RCE and gain SYSTEM privileges (Format: CVE-XXXX-XXXXX)
On page 18, we learn about PrintNightmare (CVE-2021-34527), which "permits an unprivileged user to remotely obtain elevated privileges on any system running the print spooler service, which is enabled by default. It abuses a vulnerability in how the print spooler service fails to properly authenticate users attempting to load a printer driver dynamic link library (DLL). This zero day affected all editions of Windows, allowing code execution with local SYSTEM-level privileges."

Question 5) Mention the 2 adversary groups that leverage SEO to gain initial access (Format: Group1, Group2)
On page 29, we read: "Adversaries behind both Gootkit and Yellow Cockatoo abuse search engine optimization (SEO) to display malicious content at the top of a victim’s
search results."

Question 6) In the detection rule, what should be mentioned as parent process if we are looking for execution of malicious js files [Hint: Not CMD] (Format: ParentProcessName.exe)
Starting on page 51, we learn about JavaScript executing from a ZIP file and making external network connections. Looking down just a tad, we see:
process == ​​wscript.exe
&&
command_line_includes ( .zip && ​​.js )
So the answer would be wscript.exe.

Question 7) Ransomware gangs started using affiliate model to gain initial access. Name the precursors used by affiliates of Conti ransomware group (Format: Affiliate1, Affiliate2, Afilliate3)
At the bottom of page 11, we see a nice table that tells us the answer(s) are: Qbot, Bazar, IcedID.

Question 8) The main target of coin miners was outdated software. Mention the 2 outdated software mentioned in the report (Format: Software1, Software2)
At the top of page 34, we learn: "Many of the coinminers we saw exploited flaws in outdated applications like JBoss and WebLogic [...]"

Question 9) Name the ransomware group which threatened to conduct DDoS if they didn't pay ransom (Format: GroupName)
At the bottom of page 12, we see: "An adversary known as Fancy Lazarus (no affiliation with Fancy Bear or Lazarus Group) extorted victims by threatening to conduct a distributed denial of service (DDoS) intrusion if they didn’t pay."

Question 10) What is the security measure we need to enable for RDP connections in order to safeguard from ransomware attacks? (Format: XXX)
At the top of page 13, we read: "Additionally, internet-facing remote desktop protocol (RDP) connections without multi-factor authentication (MFA) are a common ransomware vector, making MFA for any accounts that can log in via RDP a high priority."

And there we have it - some good ol' open source intelligence (OSI) teaching us what we need to be able to build a better SOC environment!
